    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d802">

                <pb facs="1056" n="940" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg940"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">822.61334/93: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Bading</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-09-11T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 11,
                                1923—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>12. Department informed that a bill was introduced in Ecuadorean Senate August
                    30, proposing reduction of the present three sucre export tax on cocoa [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">cacao</hi>] to one sucre. You will inform the Ecuadorean
                    Government that the Department relies upon President Tamayo’s assurance
                    contained in his letter of February 5, 1922, to former Minister Hartman, a copy
                    of which was transmitted with Legation’s despatch 787, February 9, 1922,<anchor
                        n="25" xml:id="d802-f1"/> in which President <pb facs="1057" n="941"
                        xml:id="pg941"/> Tamayo states he will make effort to see that the
                    indebtedness of the Asociacion de Agricultores del Ecuador to the Mercantile
                    Bank of the Americas will be totally paid under the law of 1921 which extended
                    the period of the three sucre tax on cocoa to December 31, 1925, and that if in
                    1925, this indebtedness of the Assoeiacion de Agricultores del Ecuador to the
                    Mercantile Bank of the Americas has not been paid, said tax will be extended
                    until this debt has been cancelled.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
